00:00:05.359 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
 
was a healthy  and active nine-year-old so why did she  become so unwell  and later die from a severe asthma  attack  it's a question her mom rosamund who  arrived at southwark  coroner's court with her two children  has spent the last seven years  trying to get answered i knew that i  would get to the bottom of it  i didn't imagine this though this isn't  what i imagined how it's panned out  i thought i would get an answer maybe i  thought it would be really simple  i didn't realize it was going to be this  complicated in the three years before  ella died in 2013  she was taken to hospital on nearly 30  occasions with breathing difficulties  she also suffered multiple seizures  at an inquest today a coroner ruled that  air pollution  did contribute to her death for her  is finally getting justice i think  myself and her consultants we had an  issue with the word  respiratory failure respiratory failure  could be a hundred-year-old  sits down to watch tv and suddenly dies  and they'll put  on their death certificate respiratory  failure i didn't think respiratory  failure  told us what really happened to her you  don't get a child  suddenly become that ill out of the blue  from nowhere  and then they die there has to be a  reason the question is  can you find that reason sometimes guess  what in life you can't always  i knew there was something but i didn't  know what  ella's home was 25 meters from the south  circular  in southeast london a major ring road  notorious for pollution  and often clogged with heavy traffic the  coroner said  dangerous levels of nitrogen dioxide  near to where she lived  exceeded world health organization and  eu guidelines  and her untimely death is due to the  toxic air she breathed  this is the day ella's family have spent  the last seven years fighting for  the coroner here has made legal history  by ruling for the first time ever  that air pollution is what caused the  school girl's death  and for campaigners today's verdict sets  the precedent for future cases  this is a once in a generation legal  result  it has massive ramifications across all  aspects of of all of our  lives i think we're going to see action  to reduce  vehicle emissions but not just those  because what this case has highlighted  is air pollution from other sources such  as construction  agriculture many different things i  think this is a  very special moment and rosamund has  done a wonderful thing  for many many millions of people  for ella's mom it was too late to save  her daughter  but she hopes if air pollution is  treated with the urgency that it should  be  then more lives can be saved no phillips  sky news in south london

